{ignore}
<p>The content between <code>{if}</code> and <code>{/if}</code> renders when the condition is
true. Inside it, <code>{elseif condition}</code> starts a branch that is tried when every
branch before it failed, and <code>{else}</code> the branch that renders when none held. Only
one branch ever renders.</p>

<p>The condition is a PAD expression: fields (<code>$count</code>), literals, the comparison
operators <code>eq ne gt lt ge le</code> (or <code>== != &gt; &lt; &gt;= &lt;=</code>),
<code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code>, <code>range (a, b)</code>,
<code>in ('a', 'b')</code>, <code>like</code> and <code>matches</code>. A bare value is tested
for its truth - write a comparison when a value is meant: <code>{if $count eq 0}</code>.</p>

<p>A property of an enclosing loop reads as a value in the condition:
<code>{if first@items}</code>, <code>{if current@items eq 2}</code>.</p>

<p>Under the strict syntax check (the default) a field that does not exist is an error rather
than an empty value, so a typo in a condition is reported instead of silently taking the
else branch.</p>
{/ignore}
